Umm, should we give more honor of place to the
players who get themselves spoiled beyond all hope
of playing the game for joy, and thus march through
the dungeon like General Sherman through Georgia, or
should we instead reward those who find a new way to
use an old game feature to vastly improve their
playing chances of success, and thus win by wit
rather than by rumor?

As broken as the NetHack score system has now been
demonstrated to be, what is broken about it is *not*
that it rewards the patience game style of play.

I don't see anyone whining that extinctionists get
an unfair amount of score table reward for boringly
killing off every instance of every extinctable
monster in the game, yet this is just as much an
exploit of game features (reverse genocide was
intended to be a bane, not a boon) meant for other
uses as is pudding farming.

Could we give this old wheeze a rest and get on to
the important stuff, like all the places the
existing code is marked with "I wonder if this is a
bug after all?" comments?

IMNSHO

xanthian.

Who would like to see, but does not expect to see
unless I write it myself:

about twenty more alternative Sokoban levels added,
so that memorizing the entire set of solutions isn't
so trivial;

a parameter setting in the defaults file to cater
for a much larger dungeon, since most
implementations already cater for the dungeon
extending off the physical screen, and the current
dungeon level size is much too easily explored --
I'd like merely getting to DLVL 2 via a stairway at
all be a huge challenge -- so big a challenge that
the naive player plays for hours thinking the game
is a single level game;

a rewrite of the code in Java, so that a single code
base and GUI and tile file format suffices for all
playing environments;

the "redo" capability extended to long boring sets
of repeated keystrokes, via a "start capture, stop
capture, re-use" mechanism, so that searching a chest
for traps repeatedly eight times per chest wouldn't
cause physical pain;

a multi-resolution display for graphics mode NetHack
(like many alternative NetHack displays already
have) so that large tiles could be used, yet some
level of overall viewpoint of the level be available
simultaneously.

By the way, I recently upgraded from a 1024x768
screen to a roughly HDTV format 1280x800 screen. I
can't begin to describe how nice it is to have the
whole dungeon level fit onscreen (barely) in the
default tile set, for those of you with spare
self-gifting money available.



